  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1159 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThis Afternoon into Sunday Morning: Rain Expected\n\n16z WV satellite shows a large scale trough over the Rockies, riding \nover the eastern CONUS, and southwesterly flow aloft over the CWA \nwith a jet streak just to our northwest over central MN. At the \nsurface, axis of southerly moist advection is apparent stretching \nfrom central MN all the way to TX. A cold front is slowly advancing \nsoutheastward through central MN and northeast IA.\n\nThis afternoon into tomorrow morning, as weak disturbances eject \nnortheast aloft and lift along the slow moving cold front occurs \nwithin a regime of continued moist advection, expect rain to develop \nand expand across the area. While observed 21.12z PWAT at MPX is a \nclimatological maximum for November 21st in a record stretching back \nto 1948, it is only 1.02\". This, combined with low instability \n(21.12z REFS maximum is around 100 J/kg), points to modest rainfall \nrates and a very low flooding risk. That said, across the many hours \nof rain, 21.12z REFS and 21.13z NBM 10th percentile values suggest a \nswath roughly around a Mason City-Charles City-Abbostford-Medford \npolygon will very likely (90%) see at least 1\" of rain with a \nreasonable localized maximum per REFS PMM output of a bit over 2\". \nFor areas outside of this polygon, particularly areas to the east as \nrain residence time looks to be lower as the cold front accelerates \nthrough, amounts will tend to be around 0.25-0.75\".\n\nSunday through Friday: Cooler with More Seasonable Temperatures\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage Sunday morning, strong CAA will \nlead to much cooler and rather brisk conditions with highs in the \n40s and northwesterly gusts to around 30 mph. With a turn toward \nlower heights aloft continuing through the next week, temperatures \nlook to be near or even cooler than normal (lower 40s) for late \nNovember. After a strong upper wave dives southeast over WI Monday, \nsurface high builds overnight, resulting in clearing skies and light \nwinds. The end result should be the chilliest night of the nascent \ncold season with lows having a chance (25%) to drop into the single \ndigits around Black River Falls and Tomah.",
